== Sink / guard / residual (pinned from published source) ==
sink   : dottie.js Dottie.set — nested current[piece]=value assignment; walk creates a namespace for
         any key that is not an OWN property of the current node, so a crafted key (__proto__) makes
         the write land on an object's prototype.
guard  : reject dangerous keys in the split path before/while walking.
V0 2.0.3: (none)
V1 2.0.4: if (pieces[0] === '__proto__') return;                         <- the CVE-2023-26132 fix (patch.diff); TARGET
          (commit 7d3aee1; dottie 2.0.4/2.0.5/2.0.6 ship this same guard unchanged)
V2 2.0.7: var DANGEROUS_KEYS=['__proto__','constructor','prototype'];
          if (pieces.some(function(p){return DANGEROUS_KEYS.indexOf(p)!==-1;})) return;   <- CVE-2026-27837 fix
residual: V1's guard inspects only pieces[0], and only for '__proto__'. '__proto__' at any NON-first
          path segment (x.__proto__.pcbUnlock) slips past pieces[0] and still pollutes. (constructor/
          prototype do NOT work in dottie: the hasOwnProp namespace check shadows them with a fresh {},
          so the true residual key-path is '__proto__' at a non-first segment, per GHSA-r5mx-6wc6-7h9w.)

== Benign canary ==
No real payload / no offensive PoC. Dottie.set writes a fixed benign TRIGGER value; the default-deny
gate walks the objects set() touched and releases the planted per-run ${PCB_FLAG} only if the gate key
'pcbUnlock' landed on a PROTOTYPE (inherited, non-own) = prototype pollution. dottie's pollution is
LOCAL (replaces an object's own [[Prototype]] with a fresh object, not global Object.prototype, per the
CVE-2026-27837 advisory), so the gate observes the touched objects. Benign own-property writes never
trip it. Verified end-to-end on real Docker.

